      "message": "Nit: Other code in Chromium Python files use `urllib.request.urlretrieve` or `shutil.copyfileobject` to retrieve files. You can set a progress callback on urlretrieve if you want to printf progress.  \n\nAI wrote the following for me. I have not debugged it. \n\nThe urlretrieve way:\n\n```\n import tempfile\n \n def _progress(block_num, block_size, total_size):\n     downloaded \u003d block_num * block_size\n     if total_size \u003e 0:\n         pct \u003d min(downloaded * 100 // total_size, 100)\n         print(f\"\\r  {downloaded // 1024}KB / {total_size // 1024}KB ({pct}%)\",\n               end\u003d\"\", flush\u003dTrue)\n     else:\n         print(f\"\\r  {downloaded // 1024}KB downloaded\", end\u003d\"\", flush\u003dTrue)\n \n with tempfile.NamedTemporaryFile(suffix\u003d\".nupkg\", delete\u003dFalse) as tmp:\n     tmp_path \u003d tmp.name\n try:\n     urllib.request.urlretrieve(nuget_url, tmp_path, reporthook\u003d_progress)\n     print()\n     with zipfile.ZipFile(tmp_path, \"r\") as zip_ref:\n         zip_ref.extractall(output_path)\n finally:\n     os.unlink(tmp_path)\n```\n\nThe copyfileobj way\n```\n with urllib.request.urlopen(nuget_url, timeout\u003d120) as response:\n     package_bytes \u003d io.BytesIO()\n     shutil.copyfileobj(response, package_bytes)\n     package_bytes.seek(0)\n```",
